{"id":"tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-8233516149842295530.post-943311986945336520","year":"2025","month":"11","slug":"orthogonal-projections","path":"/2025/11/orthogonal-projections.html","title":"Orthogonal Projections Explained: The Intuitive Guide for Developers","published":"2025-11-06T15:30:00.001Z","updated":"2025-11-06T15:30:00.110Z","labels":["Machine Learning","Mathematics"],"excerpt":"A developer's guide to Orthogonal Projections. Learn the math behind deconstructing a vector into its components with a step-by-step Python example.","readingMinutes":4,"youtubeId":"VrFI3xvhRQ4","youtubeThumb":true,"coverImage":null,"headings":[{"id":"how-to-deconstruct-any-vector-a-developer-s-guide-to-orthogonal-projections","text":"How to Deconstruct Any Vector (A Developer's Guide to Orthogonal Projections)","level":2},{"id":"the-mystery-finding-the-shadow","text":"The Mystery: Finding the \"Shadow\"","level":2},{"id":"the-clue-that-unlocks-everything","text":"The Clue That Unlocks Everything","level":2},{"id":"the-payoff-the-orthonormal-shortcut","text":"The Payoff: The Orthonormal Shortcut","level":2},{"id":"in-code-the-numpy-way","text":"In Code: The NumPy Way","level":2},{"id":"conclusion","text":"Conclusion","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your Turn...","level":3}],"html":"<article>\n  <h2 id=\"how-to-deconstruct-any-vector-a-developer-s-guide-to-orthogonal-projections\">\n    How to Deconstruct Any Vector (A Developer's Guide to Orthogonal Projections)\n  </h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>Finding the coordinates of a vector seems simple. In a standard Cartesian grid, the vector `v = [3, 2]` clearly has coordinates (3, 2). But what happens when our world is skewed? What if our basis vectors aren't at a perfect 90-degree angle? Suddenly, a simple question becomes a much harder puzzle.</p>\n    <p>This puzzle—finding the components of a vector in an arbitrary coordinate system—is at the heart of many applications in engineering and data science. The tool we use to solve it is one of the most powerful and intuitive concepts in linear algebra: the <b>Orthogonal Projection</b>.</p>\n    <p>In this post, we'll decode the math of projections not by memorizing a formula, but by deriving it from a single, powerful geometric clue.</p>\n    <p>Watch the video for the full visual story, then scroll down for the detailed derivation and code.</p>\n    <div>\n      <div class=\"video-embed\"><iframe allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" allowfullscreen=\"\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VrFI3xvhRQ4?si=_cfiO-ANk_rAgLnj\" title=\"YouTube video player\" loading=\"lazy\"></iframe></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"the-mystery-finding-the-shadow\">The Mystery: Finding the \"Shadow\"</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>Let's simplify our problem. We have a vector <b>v</b>, and we want to find out \"how much\" of it points in an arbitrary direction, represented by a vector <b>b</b>.</p>\n    <p>Geometrically, we are looking for the point on the line defined by <b>b</b> that is <i>closest</i> to the tip of <b>v</b>. This \"closest point\" defines a vector that looks like the \"shadow\" of <b>v</b> cast onto <b>b</b>. We call this shadow the <b>projection vector</b>, which we'll label <b>p</b>.</p>\n    <p>The key insight is that because <b>p</b> lies on the line of <b>b</b>, it must be a scaled version of <b>b</b>. Our entire mission is to find that scaling factor, `c`.</p>\n    <div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = c\\,b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n  </div>\n  \n  <h2 id=\"the-clue-that-unlocks-everything\">The Clue That Unlocks Everything</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>The fact that the projection is the \"closest\" point gives us the crucial clue we need. The line connecting the shadow <b>p</b> back to the original vector <b>v</b> must be perfectly perpendicular (orthogonal) to the direction <b>b</b>.</p>\n    <p>Let's define this connecting vector as the \"error\" vector, <b>e</b> = <b>v</b> - <b>p</b>. The geometric definition of an orthogonal projection tells us that:</p>\n    <div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b \\cdot e = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n    <p>This is it. This is the single piece of information we need to solve the entire puzzle. By substituting our definitions for <b>e</b> and <b>p</b>, we can now deduce the formula for the unknown scalar `c`.</p>\n    <ol>\n      <li><span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b \\cdot (v - p) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span></span>      (Substitute <b>e</b> = <b>v</b> - <b>p</b>)</li>\n      <li><span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b \\cdot (v - cb) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span></span>    (Substitute <b>p</b> = c<b>b</b>)</li>\n      <li><span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(b \\cdot v) - c(b \\cdot b) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span></span>   (Using the distributive property of the dot product)</li>\n      <li><span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c = \\frac{b \\cdot v}{b \\cdot b}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.2251em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8801em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">b</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">b</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">b</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span>       (Solve for c)</li>\n    </ol>\n    <p>And there we have it. The unknown coordinate `c` is simply the dot product of the original vector and the axis, divided by the dot product of the axis with itself. Now we can write the full formula for the projection vector <b>p</b>:</p>\n    <div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = \\frac{v \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}\\, b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.0574em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"the-payoff-the-orthonormal-shortcut\">The Payoff: The Orthonormal Shortcut</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>This formula is our universal \"information extractor.\" But it gets even better. What happens if our direction vector <b>b</b> is a <b>unit vector</b> from an orthonormal basis?</p>\n    <p>In that case, the length (or norm) of <b>b</b> is 1. The dot product of a vector with itself, <code><b>b</b> ⋅ <b>b</b></code>, is equal to the square of its norm. So, for a unit vector, <span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b \\cdot b = 1^2 = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span>.</p>\n    <p>The entire denominator in our formula disappears!</p>\n    <div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mtext>//&nbsp;When&nbsp;</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = (v \\cdot b)\\, b \\qquad \\text{// When } \\|b\\| = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">//&nbsp;When&nbsp;</span></span><span class=\"mord\">∥</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mord\">∥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n    <p>This is a massive engineering win. It's why we care so much about orthonormal bases: they simplify the math of projections, leading to huge gains in speed and numerical stability in algorithms that perform billions of these calculations.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"in-code-the-numpy-way\">In Code: The NumPy Way</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>The formula we derived translates directly into clean, simple Python code using NumPy.</p>\n    <pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#F97583\">import</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> numpy </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">as</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> np</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">v </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> np.array([</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">])</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">b </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> np.array([</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">4</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">0</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">]) </span><span style=\"color:#6A737D\"># The direction vector we are projecting onto</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#6A737D\"># Calculate the scaling factor 'c' using our derived formula</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">c </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> np.dot(v, b) </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">/</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> np.dot(b, b)</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#6A737D\"># Calculate the projection vector 'p'</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">p </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> c </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">*</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> b</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(</span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"The original vector v is: </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">v</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)</span>\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(</span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"The projection of v onto b is: </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">p</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)</span>\n<span style=\"color:#6A737D\"># Output: The projection of v onto b is: [2. 0.]</span></code></pre>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>By starting with a simple but frustrating problem, we've uncovered the logic behind one of linear algebra's most fundamental tools. The Orthogonal Projection is our method for deconstructing any vector into its components along any set of axes.</p>\n    <p>Now that we have this powerful tool, it opens up a new question: How can we use it, over and over, to take a messy, skewed basis and systematically \"clean it up\" into a perfect orthonormal one? That's the topic for our next deep dive.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 id=\"your-turn\">Your Turn...</h3>\n  <div>\n    <p>Can you think of another real-world scenario, besides a GPS, where deconstructing a vector into its components would be useful? Share your ideas in the comments!</p>\n  </div>\n  <div>\n    <p><i>This post is part of the \"Linear Algebra: The Core of Machine Learning\" series. For the previous part, check out: <a href=\"/2025/10/orthogonality-orthonormal-basis.html\">Upgrade Your Basis: The Power of Orthogonality</a>.</i></p>\n  </div>\n</article>"}